INT. DECOMMISSIONED PLANETARIUM - NIGHT

A domed room, round and hollow. Rows of tilted velvet seats facing a dead star-projector, a great black insect crouched at the center. Dust hangs in the beam of a single flashlight. Somewhere, a pipe drips.

OLIVER FAIRBANKS, 30s, in a corduroy jacket and glasses, plays the flashlight across the ceiling. Painted constellations peel in long grey tongues. Behind him, BERTRAND KOWALIK, 40s, heavyset, holds a tripod and an EMF meter, winded from the stairs. HESTER PRYCE, 50s, tiny, a medium's shawl over a cardigan, stands at the edge of the circle of light, eyes closed. CLEMENTINE ROY, 20s, a graduate student with a clipboard and a camera, hangs back by the door.

OLIVER
Bertrand, set it up on the projector.

BERTRAND
You want it right on the thing?

OLIVER
Where else would a ghost go?

BERTRAND
(setting down the tripod)
I don't think ghosts like to be told.

HESTER
(eyes still shut)
It's already told you.

CLEMENTINE
Told us what?

HESTER
To be careful.

OLIVER
Of what?

HESTER
Of the stars.

Silence. The pipe drips. Oliver chuckles, brief and dry, and swings the beam at the dome.

OLIVER
They're painted, Hester.

HESTER
They were painted over other stars.

Clementine lifts her camera. Frames the ceiling. Snaps. The flash fills the dome; for an instant every painted star blazes white.

CLEMENTINE
There's something in the shot.

OLIVER
Show me.

She tilts the screen toward him. In the tiny image, high in the dome, a figure hangs, arms spread, hair drifting as if underwater.

BERTRAND
That's a trick of the flash.

OLIVER
It's a reflection.

CLEMENTINE
Of what?

OLIVER
Of... us.

BERTRAND
There are only four of us.

CLEMENTINE
In the photo there are five.

Oliver takes the camera. Counts. Counts again. Swallows.

OLIVER
Bertrand, check the meter.

BERTRAND
Already pinned to red. It's been pinned since the stairs.

HESTER
(opening her eyes)
Turn off the light.

OLIVER
We're not turning off the light.

HESTER
Oliver, turn it off.

BERTRAND
Maybe she's right.

OLIVER
She's not right. She's a charlatan with a shawl.

HESTER
(softly)
Yes. And she's still right.

The flashlight flickers. Dims. Brightens. The projector, which has been dead for forty years, begins to hum. A dull amber glow swells in its eyes.

Everyone steps back. Clementine grabs Hester's sleeve. Bertrand drops the tripod, which clangs across the floor.

BERTRAND
Oliver, I'm done. I'm leaving.

OLIVER
Stay where you are.

BERTRAND
I said I'm leaving.

He turns to run. The door slams shut. The handle rattles under Clementine's hand.

CLEMENTINE
It's locked!

HESTER
It was never open.

The projector HUMS louder. A beam sweeps the dome: a slow wheel of constellations, bright, strange, wrong. Stars the wrong colors. Stars in the wrong places. A new constellation burns into the center, a shape like four small figures in a circle.

A fifth point of light appears, hovering, above and behind them.

OLIVER
(whispering)
Nobody move.

BERTRAND
(whispering)
Which one is he looking at?

OLIVER
Quiet.

BERTRAND
Which one?

HESTER
The one who counted.

Oliver lowers the camera. He doesn't breathe. The fifth star pulses once, slow as a heartbeat, and begins to descend.

CLEMENTINE
(barely audible)
Please.

The beam goes out. Darkness, total. The drip. Then, very close behind them all, the quiet scrape of a velvet seat folding down, as if someone had just sat to watch.
